Zangband now cross-compiles to a 64-bit Windows executable from this Linux box.
The build finishes with no errors, but I haven't run the game on Windows.
There's no Windows machine or Wine here, so that's the next thing to check.

How to build

  1. Install the MinGW-w64 cross-compiler once: sudo apt install gcc-mingw-w64-x86-64. I couldn't run apt here because sudo is blocked in my sandbox, so I tested with a downloaded copy of
     the llvm-mingw toolchain in my scratch space. The makefile should work with either one.
  2. Build and copy the exe into place:
  cd src
  make -f makefile.mgw          # builds src/zangband.exe
  make -f makefile.mgw install  # copies it to the top level, next to lib/
     For a 32-bit build, add CROSS=i686-w64-mingw32-.
  3. To ship it, zip zangband.exe together with the lib/ directory. The fonts, tiles and sounds in lib/xtra/ are already there. The exe only uses DLLs that come with Windows 10, so nothing
     else needs installing.

  What changed

  - New src/makefile.mgw: based on the file list in the old Borland makefile. It also builds the icon/menu resources. It puts Windows object files in src/obj-win/ so they don't mix with the
    Linux .o files that sit next to the sources. It uses the Lua binding files already in the repo instead of regenerating them.
  - Fix in src/main-win.c: the Windows interface stored a pointer to each window's data in a 4-byte slot (GetWindowLong/SetWindowLong, cbWndExtra = 4). On 64-bit Windows pointers are 8
    bytes, so this would have cut them in half and crashed as soon as a window opened. It now uses Get/SetWindowLongPtr with a pointer-sized slot. A small fallback keeps the old Borland
    build compiling.
  - .gitignore: now ignores *.exe and src/obj-win/.

  I also checked the rest of the code for 64-bit size problems. The remaining warnings are harmless, and savefiles aren't affected because long stays 32-bit on Windows.

  Nothing is committed yet. Some untracked dotfiles have appeared at the repo root (.bashrc, .zshrc, .gitconfig and others). I didn't create them on purpose; they're probably left over from
  my sandbox. Please check them before running git add -A.
