I added scripts/release-win.sh, which builds the game for Windows and packages it with lib/. I ran it and it produced dist/zangband-2.7.6-win64.zip (about 6 MB, 386 entries). The game
  still hasn't been run on Windows.

  scripts/release-win.sh                           # → dist/zangband-2.7.6-win64.zip
  CROSS=i686-w64-mingw32- scripts/release-win.sh   # → dist/zangband-2.7.6-win32.zip

  What the script does:
  1. Does a clean rebuild with src/makefile.mgw, so objects from a previous 32-bit or 64-bit build don't get reused.
  2. Reads the version from src/defines.h, following the same rules the game uses.
  3. Copies files into a temporary folder, then zips it as zangband-<ver>-<arch>/. The zip contains zangband.exe, readme.txt (renamed from readme so Windows opens it), z_faq.txt,
     z_update.txt and lib/.

  What it leaves out of lib/:
  - lib/save/1000.Ryan: your local savefile.
  - Scores (lib/apex/*.raw) and anything in lib/bone and lib/user: local player data. The empty folders are still in the zip, since the game writes into them.
  - lib/data/*.raw: these are data caches compiled on Linux. The 64-bit Linux build stores some values at a different size than Windows, so they would be the wrong format there. The game
    rebuilds them from lib/edit/*.txt when they're missing (src/init2.c:497-599).
  - makefile.zb files: only used by the Unix build.
  - lib/script/tk: only used by the Tk version of the game. The Lua scripts in lib/script are included.

  I also added dist/ to .gitignore. The script needs zip and a MinGW cross-compiler on your PATH. The Linux build's .o files were left alone. None of this is committed yet.



